{
  "version": 1,
  "name": "Reference adder · clocked code bar",
  "description": "Reference two-bit adder (feasibility witness), computed by the crank. (1) Sum: an offset slider-crank moves a drive carriage left; through a preloaded clutch spring it carries the sum bar, whose whiffletree (rockers A and B, averaged by rocker C) ends in four ball knobs that stop on the input blocks' right faces. Arms 2:1 weight bit 1 twice bit 0 and C averages the two words, so the sum bar rests at x = -8.7 + (a+b)/6: the carry is this addition of displacements. After the knobs land the clutch compresses so the crank keeps turning; a dashpot paces the stroke and damped, counterweighted rockers land square. (2) Code: an inclined ideal slot makes the code bar right of the outputs drop as far as the sum bar travelled, putting one of seven levels in front of each output's nub; each level has a tooth (that sum bit is 0) or a gap (1). (3) Probe: near the carriage's dead centre a roller pin on the crank enters a curved channel on the probe slider and drives it 0.95 right; springs on the slider push each nub toward the code bar. A tooth stops it at 0.10 (reads 0); a gap lets it run to 0.85 (reads 1). The channel ends in a dwell arc and the crank stops just past dead centre, so nothing back-drives once the torque is removed. (4) Reset: the reverse stroke pulls the slider and, through lost-motion links, the outputs back, then hooks the sum bar home; a fixed comb squares the rockers. Energy: the crank does all the motion work (about 8 J per operation); the 3.3 J of initial spring energy is mostly the clutch preload, held against a stop. Generated by tools/reference-adder.ts.",
